@@ -8,5 +8,5 @@ export const aPartialMockModuleFactoryPoisonsEveryLaterTestFile = {
   claim:
     "Bun's `mock.module` replaces a specifier in the module registry for the whole process and is never undone between test files. A factory returning fewer exports than the real module therefore breaks every file that later imports one of the missing names. The failure surfaces in the victim rather than in the file that mocked: a test file that is green alone fails inside a sweep with a SyntaxError naming an export nothing ever removed from its source, and the sweep names the file that broke rather than the file that broke it. Five of nineteen factories were of that kind and are repaired. A second fault is larger and no shape rule can see it: a factory naming every export still answers with the mocking test's values for every later importer, process-wide, because `mock.module` merges into the module record and a later factory cannot remove a key an earlier one installed. `media-page.module.test.ts` spreads the real namespace and still costs `alan/web` 59 tests. No check judges either at any phase.",
   evidence:
-    "Measured 2026-09-05, at HEAD after 3fd02863f4.\n\nSCALE. Five test files call `mock.module`, at nineteen call sites: `alan/web/alan-auth-provider/alan-auth-provider.module.test.tsx` (11), `alan/web/media-page/media-page.module.test.ts` (3), `alan/web/device-secret-sync/device-secret-sync.module.test.tsx` (2), `temper/temper-watcher/watcher-token-check/watcher-token-check.module.test.ts` (2), `alan/tracking/daily/day-place/day-place.module.test.ts` (1). Comparing each factory against the real module's runtime namespace rather than looking for a spread: five were partial, three in `alan-auth-provider` (`use-app-version-check` 1 of 3, `pages-ui-store/singleton` 2 of 7, `offline-cache-namespace` 1 of 4) and both in `device-secret-sync` (`capacitor-bridge` 2 of 8, `api-fetch` 1 of 2). All five are repaired at 0ed4a4d4a0 and 56d0bc308a. Nine more name the whole namespace by hand and were never partial. `watcher-token-check` has none, and its `unreached()` stubs are a stronger contract than a spread would be, since spreading replaces each guard with the real function and deletes the assertion.\n\nTWO INSTANCES, BOTH TODAY. `media-page.module.test.ts:21` mocked `@akasha/pages-system-service/calling` with a factory giving `askingFor`, `originOf` and `shapeFor`; that module also exports `readingFor` and `writingFor`. Line 46 mocked `@akasha/pages-access/get` with a factory giving `getPage`; that module exports six names. Both broke later files in the sweep. Repaired at 3fd02863f4 by spreading the real namespace into each factory, which took the 24-file group from 186 tests to 226. Separately, `seat-system/subagents/presence/subagent-presence.module.test.ts` landed at 9e5dcb43bf and carries the same signature: 32 tests ran and 32 were green when the file ran alone, and the file would not load inside the group.\n\nWHAT IT COST. The `writingFor` symptom was misattributed three times before the cause was found. Twice to named commits found with `git log -S`, which follows a path rather than content and so reported pure-move commits; the `Checks-bypassed` trailer on one of them made the wrong answer look well evidenced. Once to the function having been deleted from a file that never lost it: `pages/system-service/page-calling/page-calling.module.code.ts` holds `readingFor` at line 168 and `writingFor` at line 185, and the blob at the move commit already held both. No amount of reading the victim could have reached a cause three directories away, because the cause is not in any file the victim imports.\n\nA LATENT COLLISION. Two files mock `@akasha/pages-access/get`: `media-page.module.test.ts:46` and `watcher-token-check.module.test.ts:24`. Whichever runs first inside a group decides what every later importer of that specifier sees.\n\nWHAT WOULD NOT JUDGE IT. A syntax rule requiring the factory to spread the real module was the obvious guard, and it is refuted. `media-page.module.test.ts` spreads and still poisons: its stub answers `{rows: []}` for every page type but `page-type`, for the whole process, and `alan/web` runs 107 tests of which 48 are green, the 59 failures being four files each green alone. A shape rule reads the namespace and cannot read the values. Whatever judges this has to reach behaviour, or the hazard has to be removed rather than guarded.\n\nNOT MEASURED. I did not establish whether any of the thirteen partial factories is breaking a file right now, only that two did today. I did not check whether bun offers a scoped or self-restoring form of `mock.module`; if one exists, removing the hazard would be a better repair than guarding it, and this finding would be the wrong thing to act on.",
+    "Measured 2026-09-05, at HEAD after 3fd02863f4.\n\nSCALE. Five test files call `mock.module`, at nineteen call sites: `alan/web/alan-auth-provider/alan-auth-provider.module.test.tsx` (11), `alan/web/media-page/media-page.module.test.ts` (3), `alan/web/device-secret-sync/device-secret-sync.module.test.tsx` (2), `temper/temper-watcher/watcher-token-check/watcher-token-check.module.test.ts` (2), `alan/tracking/daily/day-place/day-place.module.test.ts` (1). Comparing each factory against the real module's runtime namespace rather than looking for a spread: five were partial, three in `alan-auth-provider` (`use-app-version-check` 1 of 3, `pages-ui-store/singleton` 2 of 7, `offline-cache-namespace` 1 of 4) and both in `device-secret-sync` (`capacitor-bridge` 2 of 8, `api-fetch` 1 of 2). All five are repaired at 0ed4a4d4a0 and 56d0bc308a. Nine more name the whole namespace by hand and were never partial. `watcher-token-check` has none, and its `unreached()` stubs are a stronger contract than a spread would be, since spreading replaces each guard with the real function and deletes the assertion.\n\nTWO INSTANCES, BOTH TODAY. `media-page.module.test.ts:21` mocked `@akasha/pages-service/calling` with a factory giving `askingFor`, `originOf` and `shapeFor`; that module also exports `readingFor` and `writingFor`. Line 46 mocked `@akasha/pages-access/get` with a factory giving `getPage`; that module exports six names. Both broke later files in the sweep. Repaired at 3fd02863f4 by spreading the real namespace into each factory, which took the 24-file group from 186 tests to 226. Separately, `seat-system/subagents/presence/subagent-presence.module.test.ts` landed at 9e5dcb43bf and carries the same signature: 32 tests ran and 32 were green when the file ran alone, and the file would not load inside the group.\n\nWHAT IT COST. The `writingFor` symptom was misattributed three times before the cause was found. Twice to named commits found with `git log -S`, which follows a path rather than content and so reported pure-move commits; the `Checks-bypassed` trailer on one of them made the wrong answer look well evidenced. Once to the function having been deleted from a file that never lost it: `pages/system-service/page-calling/page-calling.module.code.ts` holds `readingFor` at line 168 and `writingFor` at line 185, and the blob at the move commit already held both. No amount of reading the victim could have reached a cause three directories away, because the cause is not in any file the victim imports.\n\nA LATENT COLLISION. Two files mock `@akasha/pages-access/get`: `media-page.module.test.ts:46` and `watcher-token-check.module.test.ts:24`. Whichever runs first inside a group decides what every later importer of that specifier sees.\n\nWHAT WOULD NOT JUDGE IT. A syntax rule requiring the factory to spread the real module was the obvious guard, and it is refuted. `media-page.module.test.ts` spreads and still poisons: its stub answers `{rows: []}` for every page type but `page-type`, for the whole process, and `alan/web` runs 107 tests of which 48 are green, the 59 failures being four files each green alone. A shape rule reads the namespace and cannot read the values. Whatever judges this has to reach behaviour, or the hazard has to be removed rather than guarded.\n\nNOT MEASURED. I did not establish whether any of the thirteen partial factories is breaking a file right now, only that two did today. I did not check whether bun offers a scoped or self-restoring form of `mock.module`; if one exists, removing the hazard would be a better repair than guarding it, and this finding would be the wrong thing to act on.",
 } as const satisfies Finding
